AB  - The 59th Annual Scientific Meeting of the Nutrition Society of New Zealand was held at Tūhura Otago Museum, Dunedin (26-28 November 2025) under the theme "Advancing Nutrition: Evidence, Translation, and Action", reflecting the imperative to translate nutrition science into equitable, culturally grounded policy and practice. Scientific sessions spanned sustainable food systems, best practice in research methods and reporting, data sovereignty, women's health, sports nutrition, Māori and Pacific health, mental wellbeing, chronic disease prevention and management, and nutrition across the lifespan. Plenary presentations addressed systems approaches to Pacific food environments and policy; Māori considerations for indigenous data sovereignty in administrative and health datasets; the CONSORT-Nut extension to strengthen reporting of nutrition randomised controlled trials; menopause and cardiometabolic health; regional action on non-communicable diseases (including the MANA monitoring framework); the benefits and risks of glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists within a weight-inclusive, culturally responsive model of care; and critical appraisal of social media nutrition narratives affecting active women. The Muriel Bell Lecture, highlighted strengths-based Pacific nutrition initiatives informed by the Fonofale model of health. Workshops emphasised practical translation, including behaviour change, food waste reduction in aged residential care, weight-inclusive care for GLP-1 users, culinary nutrition, oral health, and dementia risk reduction. Overall, the conference showcased contemporary nutrition research and practice and underscored shared responsibility to turn evidence into action that improves population health and reduces inequities.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42984478/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42962966
TI  - The metabolic-brain axis: GLP-1 signaling, insulin resistance, and obesity pharmacotherapy in neurodegeneration.
AU  - Santerre Maryline
AU  - Shcherbik Natalia
AU  - Sawaya Bassel E
JO  - Metabolism: clinical and experimental
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.metabol.2026.156810
AB  - The intersection of metabolic disease and neurodegeneration represents one of the most consequential frontiers in translational neuroscience. Converging epidemiological, mechanistic, and pharmacological evidence supports a bidirectional metabolic-brain axis in which systemic insulin resistance, obesity, and chronic metabolic inflammation are associated with and may contribute to alterations in neuronal survival, synaptic plasticity, and neuroimmune homeostasis. GLP-1 receptor agonists, originally developed for glycemic control and weight management, have therefore been investigated as potential disease-modifying therapies across Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease, and related neurodegenerative conditions through mechanisms including neuronal PKA activation, microglial NLRP3 suppression, mitochondrial biogenesis, and restoration of AQP4 perivascular localization that may contribute to glymphatic waste clearance. Direct human evidence for these CNS mechanisms remains limited or absent, with current support derived primarily from preclinical models and indirect human data, as detailed in Table 1, warranting dedicated investigation in the context of GLP-1 receptor agonism. Importantly, the two pivotal phase 3 programs designed to test this hypothesis in humans - EVOKE/EVOKE+ in early Alzheimer's disease and Exenatide-PD3 in Parkinson's disease - have both now reported, and neither demonstrated a statistically significant benefit on its primary clinical endpoint, despite favorable biomarker and epidemiological signals preceding these results. This review synthesizes current knowledge on brain insulin signaling, GLP-1 neurobiology, obesity-driven neuroinflammation, and the translational evidence for metabolic therapeutics in neurodegeneration, and uses the dissociation between mechanistic plausibility, epidemiological association, and null confirmatory trial results to identify critical knowledge gaps and translational barriers related to CNS target engagement, optimal dosing, and disease-modifying efficacy of GLP-1 receptor agonists. The review is organized into four major themes: brain insulin signaling, central GLP-1 biology, obesity-associated neuroinflammation, and the translational evidence supporting metabolic therapies for neurodegenerative disease.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42962966/
ER  - 

