AB  - GLP-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) have been shown to reduce invasive interventions and improve symptom control in idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH). However, their impact on neurocognitive outcomes remains unknown. We used the TriNetX US Collaborative Network to conduct a propensity score-matched study of adults with a first IIH diagnosis from January 2016 to December 2025. The GLP-1RA cohort-initiated therapy within 1 month before to 3 months after diagnosis. Patients with CSF diversion, venous sinus stenting, or pre-existing cognitive or fatigue diagnoses were excluded. Matching balanced demographics, cardiometabolic comorbidities, IIH-directed pharmacotherapy, BMI, and HbA1c. Cognitive decline, dementia composite, fatigue, inpatient admission, and emergency department visits were assessed at 1, 3, and 5 years using Kaplan-Meier and Cox proportional hazards analyses. After matching, 3,652 patients were retained in each cohort. At 5 years, GLP-1RA use was associated with lower event probabilities for cognitive decline, 4.69% versus 7.99%, HR 0.51, 95% CI 0.38-0.69; cognitive decline or dementia, 4.88% versus 8.17%, HR 0.52, 95% CI 0.39-0.69; and fatigue, 11.79% versus 14.88%, HR 0.71, 95% CI 0.59-0.87. Event probabilities were also lower for inpatient admission, 39.24% versus 41.70%, HR 0.70, 95% CI 0.64-0.77; and emergency department visits, 29.55% versus 37.82%, HR 0.69, 95% CI 0.62-0.76. In medically managed adults with IIH, GLP-1RA therapy was associated with lower risks of incident cognitive decline, cognitive decline or dementia, fatigue, inpatient admission, and emergency department visits. These findings suggest GLP-1RAs improve neurocognitive outcomes and quality of life in IIH.
